    "https://uasys.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/UASYS/job/Little-Rock/Director-of-Alumni-Engagement_R0089249": {
      "title": "Director of Alumni Engagement",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-07-30\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\n\nCurrent University of Arkansas System employees, including student employees and graduate assistants, need to log in to Workday via MyApps.Microsoft.com , then access Find Jobs from the Workday search bar to view and apply for open positions. Students at University of Arkansas System will also view open positions and apply within Workday by searching for “Find Jobs for Students”. All Job Postings will close at 12:01 a.m. CT on the specified Closing Date (if designated). If you close the browser or exit your application prior to submitting, the application process will be saved as a draft. You will be able to access and complete the application through “My Draft Applications” located on your Candidate Home page.   Closing Date: Type of Position: Alumni Relations Workstudy Position: No Job Type: Regular Work Shift: Sponsorship Available: No Institution Name: University of Arkansas at Little Rock The University of Arkansas at Little Rock is a metropolitan research university that provides an accessible, quality education through flexible learning and unparalleled internship opportunities. At UA Little Rock, we prepare our more than 8,900 students to be innovators and responsible leaders in their fields. Committed to its metropolitan research university mission, UA Little Rock is a driving force in Little Rock's thriving cultural community and a major component of the city and state's growing profile as a regional leader in research, technology transfer, economic development, and job creation. Below you will find the details for the position including any supplementary documentation and questions you should review before applying for the opening.  To apply for the position, please click the   Apply link/button. If you have a disability and need assistance with the hiring process please contact Human Resources at 501-916-3180 . For general application assistance or if you have questions about a job posting, please contact Human Resources at 501-916-3180 . Department: Alumni Relations Department's Website: Summary of Job Duties: The Director of Alumni Engagement serves as the University's chief relationship builder for more than 150,000 former students and alumni, creating meaningful opportunities for individuals to reconnect with the institution and with one another. Reporting to the Associate Vice Chancellor for Alumni and Development, this position executes the strategic plan for alumni engagement by identifying emerging markets, cultivating volunteer leaders, developing innovative programs, and building partnerships that strengthen lifelong connections between former students, alumni, and the University. As the University enters its second century of service, the Director expands alumni engagement beyond traditional programming by meeting former students and alumni where they live, work, and lead. This position actively seeks opportunities to engage graduates in growing communities across Arkansas and across the nation, celebrating their professional accomplishments while connecting them to the people, programs, and possibilities shaping the University's future. Serving as the primary staff liaison to the Alumni Board of Directors, the Director works collaboratively with development officers, academic leadership, students, volunteers, and campus partners to transform pride in UA Little Rock into meaningful engagement, advocacy, volunteerism, mentorship, and philanthropic investment. The Director is charged with moving the needle on alumni engagement by creating meaningful opportunities, success in this role is measured not simply by the number of programs delivered or events hosted, but by the number of meaningful relationships created, strengthened, and sustained. Through data-informed strategies, innovative programming, regional outreach, volunteer leadership, and strategic partnerships, the Director continually expands the University's network of engaged alumni while deepening the quality of those relationships over time. This position actively identifies underserved alumni populations, emerging markets, geographic regions, industries, and affinity groups, developing targeted engagement strategies that broaden the University's reach and increase participation. The Director believes alumni engagement is about far more than planning events it is about creating relationships that endure across generations. The Director works to open eyes in both directions helping former students and alumni better understand the innovation, research, student success, and momentum shaping their alma mater today, while helping the University recognize, celebrate, and engage the extraordinary accomplishments of its graduates. By strengthening these connections, the Director develops a growing network of engaged ambassadors whose professional success reflects the University's impact and whose continued involvement advances its mission. Working closely with the Director of Alumni, Corporate/Foundation Relations, the National Director of Development, and the University's development team, the Director ensures alumni engagement efforts support the identification, cultivation, and stewardship of prospective donors and volunteer leaders. Every alumni interaction is viewed as an opportunity to deepen relationships, strengthen affinity, identify future philanthropic partners, and advance the University's strategic priorities. Through vision, initiative, and relationship-building, the Director will help shape an alumni engagement program that inspires lifelong connection and positions UA Little Rock for an even stronger second century of service. This position is governed by state and federal laws, and agency/institution policy. Qualifications: Required Education and/or Experience Bachelor's degree from an accredited institution; Three years of professional experience in alumni relations, advancement, development, fundraising, volunteer management, higher education, nonprofit management, or a related field. Preferred Education and/or Experience Demonstrated experience building relationships with diverse constituencies; Experience coordinating programs, projects, volunteer initiatives, or fundraising activities; Master's degree; Experience in higher education advancement, alumni relations, annual giving, or fundraising; Experience working with volunteer boards, advisory councils, or alumni organizations; Experience conducting donor discovery, qualification, or solicitation activities; Experience utilizing CRM systems for constituent engagement and reporting. Job Duties and Responsibilities Alumni Engagement and Relationship Development Develop and implement strategies to increase alumni engagement and participation across the University's alumni community, including affinity-based constituencies such as Donaghey Scholars, former student-athletes, fraternity and sorority alumni, and other priority alumni groups; Develop targeted engagement plans for key alumni affinity groups that strengthen lifelong relationships, expand volunteer leadership, increase event participation, and create pathways for philanthropic support; Identify, cultivate, and maintain relationships with alumni across diverse constituencies; Conduct a minimum of ten (10) meaningful face-to-face visits each week with alumni to cultivate lifelong relationships, increase alumni engagement, identify volunteer leaders, discover philanthropic interests, and strengthen the University's culture of philanthropy; Represent the University at alumni, community, and campus events; Maintain accurate records of alumni interactions and engagement activities within the University's CRM system. Alumni Event Engagement Collaborate with the Director of Alumni, Corporate/Foundation Relations to develop engagement strategies for alumni events and programs; Develop engagement opportunities and programming that encourage participation from priority alumni affinity groups, including Donaghey Scholars, former student-athletes, fraternity and sorority alumni, and other identified constituencies; Identify target alumni audiences and support outreach efforts to increase participation. Utilize alumni events to cultivate relationships, recruit volunteers, identify prospective donors, and strengthen alumni affinity; Coordinate alumni engagement activities associated with University events and initiatives; Evaluate event participation data and engagement outcomes to inform future strategies. Alumni Board Management Serve as the primary staff liaison to the Alumni Board of Directors; Coordinate board meetings, committee meetings, and related activities; Assist with the recruitment, onboarding, and retention of board members; Support the development of board goals, initiatives, and annual work plans; Facilitate communication between board members and University leadership; Encourage board participation in engagement and philanthropic initiatives. Volunteer Engagement Recruit, train, and manage alumni volunteers; Develop opportunities for alumni involvement through mentoring, speaking engagements, networking activities, career development programs, and University initiatives; Maintain a volunteer engagement pipeline and track volunteer participation; Collaborate with campus partners to identify meaningful volunteer opportunities for alumni. Alumni Philanthropy and Pipeline Development Promote alumni participation in annual giving and University fundraising initiatives; Conduct donor discovery and qualification visits to identify philanthropic interests and capacity; Solicit annual and leadership-level gifts from alumni and friends of the University; Identify and refer qualified major gift prospects to development staff; Collaborate with development officers to support cultivation, stewardship, and solicitation strategies; Maintain records of fundraising activity and prospect movement within the CRM. Alumni Communications and Recognition Collaborate with University communications staff to promote alumni engagement opportunities; Identify and highlight alumni achievements and success stories; Coordinate alumni recognition initiatives and outreach efforts; Support alumni communications through digital, print, and social media platforms. Collaboration and Strategic Initiatives Partner with development staff, academic units, and campus leadership to advance institutional priorities; Provide alumni engagement and participation data to support strategic planning and decision-making; Assist in the development and implementation of new alumni engagement programs and initiatives; Support special projects and strategic initiatives as assigned. Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities Knowledge of alumni relations, advancement, fundraising, volunteer management, or higher education practices; Strong interpersonal and relationship-building skills; Excellent written and verbal communication skills; Ability to recruit, motivate, and engage volunteers and donors; Ability to conduct donor discovery conversations and philanthropic outreach; Ability to manage multiple projects and priorities simultaneously; Proficiency in CRM systems and constituent engagement tracking; Strong organizational, analytical, and problem-solving skills; Ability to work independently and collaboratively in a team environment; Must have a valid driver's license. Additional Information: Salary Information: Commensurate with education and experience Required Documents to Apply: Cover Letter/Letter of Application, List of three Professional References (name, email, business title), Resume Optional Documents: Special Instructions to Applicants: Recruitment Contact Information: Kristi Smith, Associate Vice Chancellor of Alumni and Development, klsmith4@ualr.edu All application materials must be uploaded to the University of Arkansas System Career Site  https://uasys.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/UASYS   Please do not send to listed recruitment contact. Pre-employment Screening Requirements: Criminal Background Check, Financial Credit Check, Motor Vehicle Reports Check, Sex Offender Registry The University of Arkansas at Little Rock is committed to providing a safe campus community. We conduct background checks for applicants being considered for employment. Background checks include a criminal background check and a sex offender registry check. For certain positions, there may also be a financial (credit) background check, a Motor Vehicle Registry ( MVR ) check, and/or drug screening. Required checks are identified in the position listing. A criminal conviction or arrest pending adjudication or adverse financial history information alone shall not disqualify an applicant in the absence of a relationship to the requirements of the position. Background check information will be used in a confidential, non-discriminatory manner consistent with state and federal law. The University of Arkansas is an equal opportunity institution. The University does not discriminate in its education programs or activities (including in admission and employment) on the basis of any category or status protected by law, including age, race, color, national origin, disability, religion, protected veteran status, military service, genetic information, sex, sexual preference, or pregnancy. Questions or concerns about the application of Title IX, which prohibits discrimination on the basis of sex, may be sent to the University’s Title IX Coordinator and to the U.S. Department of Education Office for Civil Rights. Persons must have proof of legal authority to work in the United States on the first day of employment. All application information is subject to public disclosure under the Arkansas Freedom of Information Act. Constant Physical Activity: Sitting, Talking Frequent Physical Activity: Driving Occasional Physical Activity: N/A Benefits Eligible: Yes"
    },
    "https://uasys.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/UASYS/job/Little-Rock/Administrative-Operations-Coordinator_R0088720": {
      "title": "Administrative Operations Coordinator",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-07-22\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nFull time\n\nCurrent University of Arkansas System employees, including student employees and graduate assistants, need to log in to Workday via MyApps.Microsoft.com , then access Find Jobs from the Workday search bar to view and apply for open positions. Students at University of Arkansas System will also view open positions and apply within Workday by searching for “Find Jobs for Students”. All Job Postings will close at 12:01 a.m. CT on the specified Closing Date (if designated). If you close the browser or exit your application prior to submitting, the application process will be saved as a draft. You will be able to access and complete the application through “My Draft Applications” located on your Candidate Home page.   Closing Date: Type of Position: Staff (Non-Classified) Workstudy Position: No Job Type: Regular Work Shift: Sponsorship Available: No Institution Name: University of Arkansas at Little Rock The University of Arkansas at Little Rock is a metropolitan research university that provides an accessible, quality education through flexible learning and unparalleled internship opportunities. At UA Little Rock, we prepare our more than 8,900 students to be innovators and responsible leaders in their fields. Committed to its metropolitan research university mission, UA Little Rock is a driving force in Little Rock's thriving cultural community and a major component of the city and state's growing profile as a regional leader in research, technology transfer, economic development, and job creation. Below you will find the details for the position including any supplementary documentation and questions you should review before applying for the opening.  To apply for the position, please click the   Apply link/button. If you have a disability and need assistance with the hiring process please contact Human Resources at 501-916-3180 . For general application assistance or if you have questions about a job posting, please contact Human Resources at 501-916-3180 . Department: Student Affairs Department's Website: Summary of Job Duties: The Division of Student Affairs Administrative Operations Coordinator is an administrative support position that serves as an initial point of contact for visitors to the Office of the Vice Chancellor for Student Affairs. This position will provide administrative support, fiscal Support, and communication support. This position requires in-person, on-site work during university business hours (Monday–Friday, 8:00 a.m.–5:00 p.m.). This position is governed by state and federal laws, and agency/institution policy. Qualifications: Required Education and/or Experience The formal education equivalent of an associate degree, plus two (2) years of experience in program administration or related area. Other job-related education and/or experience may be substituted for all or part of these basic requirements. Preferred Education and/or Experience Bachelor’s degree; Experience working with a diverse population; Previous experience working with college students; Strong understanding of navigating policies and procedures, procurement, vendors, and other financial tasks. Job Duties and Responsibilities Front Desk & Administrative Support (40%) Manage front desk operations for the Office of the Vice Chancellor for Student Affairs, ensuring professional and responsive service; Serve as the primary point of contact for students, faculty, staff, parents/guardians, and visitors; Answer phones, route calls, and respond to general inquiries; Schedule meetings and assist with calendar coordination for office leadership; Coordinate meeting logistics, room reservations, and preparation of meeting materials;  Prepare and format routine correspondence, reports, and meeting materials; Maintain organized filing systems (paper and electronic) Office Operations & Coordination (35%) Coordinate daily office operations, including maintenance requests, supply procurement, and inventory tracking; Assist with organizing and maintaining office systems, records, and shared resources; Coordinate and maintain office systems, records, shared resources, and operational procedures to support efficient office operations;  Support workflow coordination and ensure timely completion of administrative tasks; Maintain internal contact lists and resource information to support office operations: Serve as an initial point of contact for student inquiries and concerns and assist with connecting students to appropriate university resources and services;  Provide general information and guidance on university services, policies, and procedures; Report student concerns to the Executive Manager for Student Affairs to ensure the  Inform the Executive Manager for Student Affairs of routine concerns and of complex or sensitive issues that require escalation. Fiscal & Administrative Support (10%) Assist with purchasing processes, including requisitions and ordering supplies; Support travel authorizations and reimbursement documentation; Maintain basic tracking of expenditures and assist with resolving routine administrative or financial issues; Provide administrative support for financial reporting processes as needed. Program & Staff Support (10%) Assist with coordinating schedules and tasks for student workers; Support onboarding processes and basic training coordination for student employees; Assist with planning and logistics for meetings, events, and divisional activities; Provide general support for employee engagement initiatives and office activities. Assist as needed with division-level communications; Coordinate the development of a regular Student Affairs newsletter; Prepare drafts, letters, memoranda, reports, etc. for final copy. Special Projects & Additional Duties ( 5%) Assist with special projects and divisional initiatives as assigned; Perform other duties as assigned. Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities Knowledge of Banner and Workday systems; Knowledge of university budget and finance policies, procedures, and operations; Knowledge of state, federal, and university rules, regulations, and procedures; Ability to work in a diverse and dynamic environment; Ability to work collaboratively and independently with common goals as a part of a team; Ability to make sound judgments and assist in resolving student concerns; Effective interpersonal communication, writing, and organizational abilities; Civility in all interactions of a public or confidential nature; Demonstrate follow-up contacts and consultations in a timely manner; Ability to coordinate the activities of campus academic and/or social programs;  Ability to evaluate collected information and formulate recommendations; Ability to compile information into report form. Additional Information: Salary Information: $30,733 Required Documents to Apply: Cover Letter/Letter of Application, List of three Professional References (name, email, business title), Resume Optional Documents: Special Instructions to Applicants: Recruitment Contact Information: Shannon Maxwell Executive Manager for the Division of Student Affairs 501-916-3328 smaxwell@ualr.edu All application materials must be uploaded to the University of Arkansas System Career Site  https://uasys.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/UASYS   Please do not send to listed recruitment contact. Pre-employment Screening Requirements: Criminal Background Check, Financial Credit Check, Sex Offender Registry The University of Arkansas at Little Rock is committed to providing a safe campus community. We conduct background checks for applicants being considered for employment. Background checks include a criminal background check and a sex offender registry check. For certain positions, there may also be a financial (credit) background check, a Motor Vehicle Registry ( MVR ) check, and/or drug screening. Required checks are identified in the position listing. A criminal conviction or arrest pending adjudication or adverse financial history information alone shall not disqualify an applicant in the absence of a relationship to the requirements of the position. Background check information will be used in a confidential, non-discriminatory manner consistent with state and federal law. The University of Arkansas is an equal opportunity institution. The University does not discriminate in its education programs or activities (including in admission and employment) on the basis of any category or status protected by law, including age, race, color, national origin, disability, religion, protected veteran status, military service, genetic information, sex, sexual preference, or pregnancy. Questions or concerns about the application of Title IX, which prohibits discrimination on the basis of sex, may be sent to the University’s Title IX Coordinator and to the U.S. Department of Education Office for Civil Rights. Persons must have proof of legal authority to work in the United States on the first day of employment. All application information is subject to public disclosure under the Arkansas Freedom of Information Act. Constant Physical Activity: Hearing, Repetitive Motion, Sitting, Talking Frequent Physical Activity: Pulling, Pushing, Reaching, Repetitive Motion, Sitting, Standing, Talking, Walking Occasional Physical Activity: Balancing, Stooping Benefits Eligible: Yes"
    },
    "https://uasys.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/UASYS/job/Little-Rock/Resource-Family-Trainer_R0088465": {
      "title": "Resource Family Trainer",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-07-16\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nFull time\n\nCurrent University of Arkansas System employees, including student employees and graduate assistants, need to log in to Workday via MyApps.Microsoft.com , then access Find Jobs from the Workday search bar to view and apply for open positions. Students at University of Arkansas System will also view open positions and apply within Workday by searching for “Find Jobs for Students”. All Job Postings will close at 12:01 a.m. CT on the specified Closing Date (if designated). If you close the browser or exit your application prior to submitting, the application process will be saved as a draft. You will be able to access and complete the application through “My Draft Applications” located on your Candidate Home page.   Closing Date: Type of Position: Professional Staff - Institutional Affairs Workstudy Position: No Job Type: Regular Work Shift: Day Shift (United States of America) Sponsorship Available: No Institution Name: University of Arkansas at Little Rock The University of Arkansas at Little Rock is a metropolitan research university that provides an accessible, quality education through flexible learning and unparalleled internship opportunities. At UA Little Rock, we prepare our more than 8,900 students to be innovators and responsible leaders in their fields. Committed to its metropolitan research university mission, UA Little Rock is a driving force in Little Rock's thriving cultural community and a major component of the city and state's growing profile as a regional leader in research, technology transfer, economic development, and job creation. Below you will find the details for the position including any supplementary documentation and questions you should review before applying for the opening.  To apply for the position, please click the   Apply link/button. If you have a disability and need assistance with the hiring process please contact Human Resources at 501-916-3180 . For general application assistance or if you have questions about a job posting, please contact Human Resources at 501-916-3180 . Department: MidSOUTH Department's Website: midsouth.ualr.edu Summary of Job Duties: The Resource Family Trainer is responsible for implementing the contracted resource and adoptive parent pre-service training curriculum and completing the home study in partnership with the Division of Children and Family Services. The Resource Family Trainer will receive training and get certified in the contracted resource and adoptive parent training program and the contracted home study model. The person in this position is responsible for the delivery of the contracted training curriculum and home study model to prospective resource and adoptive participants referred to the program for training. This position has regular contact with University personnel, DCFS staff, and other public agencies, community professionals, and the public. This position requires extensive in-state travel and work outside normal office hours. This full-time, annually renewed grant-funded position is directly responsible to the Resource Family Program Director or designee. State and federal laws and agency/institution policy govern this position. Qualifications: Required Education and/or Experience: Bachelor’s Degree in Social Work OR Master’s degree in a related field; One (1) year of professional experience working with families in the child welfare system.   Preferred Education and/or Experience: Experience in writing home assessments, psychosocial histories, training in curriculum development, and training delivery; Master’s Degree in Social Work, Psychology, Counseling, with appropriate professional licensure preferred, e.g., LMSW, LCSW, LAC, LPC; Fluent in Spanish. Job Duties and Responsibilities: Training Management: Will receive training and certification in the contracted resource and adoptive parent training program model and the contracted home study model; Facilitate the contracted pre-service training curriculum with prospective resource/adoptive parents who meet the Division's standards for approval or other resource parent pre-service training curriculum approved by DCFS, as needed; Notify the appropriate DCFS Area Directors, Area Resource Workers, and Adoption Specialists of the resource parent pre-service being offered to prospective resource/adoptive parents when requested by supervisor.   Assessment Management: Conduct the contracted home study model on prospective resource and adoptive homes in accordance with the criteria set out in the Minimum Licensing Standards for Child Welfare Agencies, Standards for Approval of Resource and Adoptive Homes, and the contracted home study model protocols; Conduct both of the minimally required visits with the applicants in the applicants' home; Provide original contracted home study model elements ( e.g., completed home study report template, questionnaires, psychosocial rating sheets, etc.) to DCFS at the completion of the home study; Submit a completed home study final report to DCFS within the contracted required days as agreed upon by DCFS; Complete all home visits assigned by the supervisor. These home visits may be assigned statewide.   Program Management:   Receive weekly supervision and problem-solve issues that may arise; Communicate with program participants (phone, email, mail) between sessions, as needed; Complete program documentation and monthly reporting requirements; Attend required monthly and quarterly meetings with Area Resource staff to provide ongoing feedback regarding training needs, problem-solving, and technical assistance.   Other duties as assigned.   Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities: Excellent skills in facilitating groups from diverse backgrounds; Skills in conducting home visits; Ability to use assessment tools/collect data; Ability to travel in-state and out-of-state with overnight stays; Knowledge of teaching principles and methods; Ability to coordinate and conduct training programs, as assigned; Ability to prepare reports, maintain records, and ensure documentation in compliance with program deliverables; The ability to work outside of normal office hours is required; Ability to adapt to organizational change and handle multiple tasks; Must have computer skills, including Microsoft Office, and the ability to operate AV equipment.   Additional Information: Salary Information: $52,500 - $55,500 Required Documents to Apply: Cover Letter/Letter of Application, List of three Professional References (name, email, business title), Resume, Unofficial/Official Transcript(s) Optional Documents: Special Instructions to Applicants: Recruitment Contact Information: Mary Tillman, Administrative Support Manager, mjtillman@midsouth.ualr.edu All application materials must be uploaded to the University of Arkansas System Career Site  https://uasys.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/UASYS   Please do not send to listed recruitment contact. Pre-employment Screening Requirements: Child Maltreatment, Criminal Background Check, Motor Vehicle Reports Check, Sex Offender Registry The University of Arkansas at Little Rock is committed to providing a safe campus community. We conduct background checks for applicants being considered for employment. Background checks include a criminal background check and a sex offender registry check. For certain positions, there may also be a financial (credit) background check, a Motor Vehicle Registry ( MVR ) check, and/or drug screening. Required checks are identified in the position listing. A criminal conviction or arrest pending adjudication or adverse financial history information alone shall not disqualify an applicant in the absence of a relationship to the requirements of the position. Background check information will be used in a confidential, non-discriminatory manner consistent with state and federal law. The University of Arkansas is an equal opportunity institution. The University does not discriminate in its education programs or activities (including in admission and employment) on the basis of any category or status protected by law, including age, race, color, national origin, disability, religion, protected veteran status, military service, genetic information, sex, sexual preference, or pregnancy. Questions or concerns about the application of Title IX, which prohibits discrimination on the basis of sex, may be sent to the University’s Title IX Coordinator and to the U.S. Department of Education Office for Civil Rights. Persons must have proof of legal authority to work in the United States on the first day of employment. All application information is subject to public disclosure under the Arkansas Freedom of Information Act. Constant Physical Activity: Hearing, Manipulate items with fingers, including keyboarding, Sitting, Talking Frequent Physical Activity: Driving, Manipulate items with fingers, including keyboarding, Standing, Walking Occasional Physical Activity: Balancing, Climbing, Crawling, Crouching, Feeling, Grasping, Kneeling, Lifting, Manipulate items with fingers, including keyboarding, Pulling, Pushing, Reaching, Stooping Benefits Eligible: Yes"
    },
    "https://uasys.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/UASYS/job/Little-Rock/Adjunct-Lecturer---Fall-2027-Artist-in-Residence_R0088035": {
      "title": "Adjunct Lecturer / Fall 2027 Artist in Residence",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-07-15\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\n\nCurrent University of Arkansas System employees, including student employees and graduate assistants, need to log in to Workday via MyApps.Microsoft.com , then access Find Jobs from the Workday search bar to view and apply for open positions. Students at University of Arkansas System will also view open positions and apply within Workday by searching for “Find Jobs for Students”. All Job Postings will close at 12:01 a.m. CT on the specified Closing Date (if designated). If you close the browser or exit your application prior to submitting, the application process will be saved as a draft. You will be able to access and complete the application through “My Draft Applications” located on your Candidate Home page.   Closing Date: Type of Position: Adjunct Faculty Workstudy Position: No Job Type: Less than Annual Appointment (Fixed Term) Work Shift: Sponsorship Available: No Institution Name: University of Arkansas at Little Rock The University of Arkansas at Little Rock is a metropolitan research university that provides an accessible, quality education through flexible learning and unparalleled internship opportunities. At UA Little Rock, we prepare our more than 8,900 students to be innovators and responsible leaders in their fields. Committed to its metropolitan research university mission, UA Little Rock is a driving force in Little Rock's thriving cultural community and a major component of the city and state's growing profile as a regional leader in research, technology transfer, economic development, and job creation. Below you will find the details for the position including any supplementary documentation and questions you should review before applying for the opening.  To apply for the position, please click the   Apply link/button. If you have a disability and need assistance with the hiring process please contact Human Resources at 501-916-3180 . For general application assistance or if you have questions about a job posting, please contact Human Resources at 501-916-3180 . Department: Art and Design Department's Website: Summary of Job Duties: The Windgate Artist in Residence will work in the Windgate Center of Art + Design at the University of Arkansas at Little Rock for the fall 2027 semester. This residency offers the Windgate Artist in Residence the opportunity to make work and to engage with the community of the UA Little Rock School of Art and Design. While in residence, the artist is expected to make work in the provided studio space and/or the shared studio spaces of the building. In addition, artists will interact with students through weekly open studio hours, studio visits to student workspaces, in-class activities hosted by program faculty, and welcome and farewell community receptions. Participating artists will also donate one work of art to the UA Little Rock Art Collection. This position is governed by state and federal laws, and agency/institution policy. Qualifications: Required Education and/or Experience: High School Diploma Preferred Education and/or Experience: MFA, BFA, BA, or commensurate experience; Evidence of active participation in exhibitions at the national or international level; Teaching and/or mentorship of students at any level. Job Duties and Responsibilities: Being present for the duration of the residency (August 15 - December 15); Making artwork in the Windgate Center of Art + Design M-F (daily); Maintaining open studio hours (weekly); Giving a public artist’s talk (one time); Critiquing students; Participating as a guest artist in relevant classes (1-2 times): examples include giving a talk, participating in critiques, or leading an in-class activity; Attending welcome and farewell receptions; Providing a work to the UA Little Rock Art Collection; Other duties as assigned. Knowledge, Skills and Abilities: Knowledge of artistic processes and media; Knowledge of studio safety procedures; Strong interpersonal skills; Ability to teach; Ability to effectively communicate and collaborate; Willingness to engage in collaboration with faculty as a guest artist in their classes; Willingness to engage with students and the broader community. Additional Information: Salary Information: Commensurate with education and experience Required Documents to Apply: Cover Letter/Letter of Application, Curriculum Vitae, List of three Professional References (name, email, business title), Other (see special instructions for details) Optional Documents: Special Instructions to Applicants: Important note: please upload all of the following files in the field on the application that prompts you to upload your CV. You may submit them individually or as one large PDF. This is the only place on the application that allows file uploads. -Letter of interest specifying qualifications and experience (cover letter) -Curriculum Vitae -PDF Portfolio of artwork or link to online portfolio Additional Information: Housing is provided approximately one block from the Windgate Center for Art + Design. The house is a 2-bedroom, 1-bathroom house with parking. Artists with families are encouraged to apply. One pet is allowed. Recruitment Contact Information: Joli Livaudais, Director, jklivaudais@ualr.edu All application materials must be uploaded to the University of Arkansas System Career Site  https://uasys.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/UASYS   Please do not send to listed recruitment contact. Pre-employment Screening Requirements: No Background Check Required The University of Arkansas at Little Rock is committed to providing a safe campus community. We conduct background checks for applicants being considered for employment. Background checks include a criminal background check and a sex offender registry check. For certain positions, there may also be a financial (credit) background check, a Motor Vehicle Registry ( MVR ) check, and/or drug screening. Required checks are identified in the position listing. A criminal conviction or arrest pending adjudication or adverse financial history information alone shall not disqualify an applicant in the absence of a relationship to the requirements of the position. Background check information will be used in a confidential, non-discriminatory manner consistent with state and federal law. The University of Arkansas is an equal opportunity institution. The University does not discriminate in its education programs or activities (including in admission and employment) on the basis of any category or status protected by law, including age, race, color, national origin, disability, religion, protected veteran status, military service, genetic information, sex, sexual preference, or pregnancy. Questions or concerns about the application of Title IX, which prohibits discrimination on the basis of sex, may be sent to the University’s Title IX Coordinator and to the U.S. Department of Education Office for Civil Rights. Persons must have proof of legal authority to work in the United States on the first day of employment. All application information is subject to public disclosure under the Arkansas Freedom of Information Act. Constant Physical Activity: Sitting, Standing Frequent Physical Activity: Talking, Walking Occasional Physical Activity: Crouching, Feeling, Grasping, Lifting, Manipulate items with fingers, including keyboarding Benefits Eligible: No"
    },
